💿 System Diagnostics & Storage Recovery — Troubleshooting Log

    Location: Huaral, Lima, Peru | Date: October 16, 2026
    Skills demonstrated: Linux system administration · Filesystem repair · Performance optimization · Permission management · Troubleshooting & pattern recognition

📋 Overview
Resolved multiple storage and boot performance issues on a Linux system, including an unmountable secondary drive, excessive boot delays, and automated maintenance tasks impacting daily usability. Diagnosed root causes, applied targeted fixes, and implemented a sustainable backup strategy — all while documenting the full diagnostic process.
🔍 Problems Identified
1. Secondary Drive Not Mounting

    1TB HDD (/dev/sdb) would not mount — filesystem errors detected
    Initial attempts showed permission/ownership conflicts post-repair

2. Extremely Slow Boot (20–30 minutes daily)

    System felt sluggish for ~30 minutes after every morning startup
    Disk utilization spiked to 90%+ with minimal actual I/O — drive appeared "stuck waiting"
    systemd-analyze blame revealed long wait times on:
        dev-sda3.device (~42s) — main SSD taking too long to become ready
        systemd-journal-flush.service (~41s) — log writes blocking readiness
        lvm2-monitor.service (~39s) — volume monitoring delaying startup
        e2scrub_reap.service (~14s) — filesystem cleanup job running every boot
    Timeshift configured with Daily + Boot snapshots — creating backups during every startup

3. Filesystem Checks Triggered After Errors

    Previous fsck run had reset mount-count thresholds, risking frequent full scans

🛠️ Solutions Applied
✅ Drive Recovery & Permissions
bash

# Repaired ext4 filesystem errors
fsck /dev/sdb

# Mounted successfully at /mnt/MainD
# Set user ownership — no more password prompts for regular file operations
sudo chown -R $USER:$USER /mnt/MainD

    Result: 933.3 GB fully accessible, read/write without repeated authentication prompts

✅ Filesystem Check Tuning
bash

# Checked current settings
tune2fs -l /dev/sda3 | grep "Maximum mount count"

# Set sensible thresholds — check every 30 boots OR every 7 days
sudo tune2fs -c 30 -i 7d /dev/sda3

    Result: No more unnecessary full scans at every startup

✅ Disabled Problematic Services
bash

# Stopped e2scrub from running every boot
sudo systemctl disable --now e2scrub_reap.service

✅ Optimized Timeshift Backup Strategy

    ❌ Boot snapshots — disabled entirely
    ❌ Daily snapshots — replaced with weekly
    ✅ Weekly snapshots — keep last 3 copies
    ✅ Created manual baseline snapshot immediately after configuration
    ✅ Snapshots stored on separate drive from active work files

